### How does CountDownLatch work?

CountDownLatch(N) starts with a count of N. Any thread can call `countDown()` to decrement. Threads calling `await()` block until the count reaches zero.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class LatchDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        CountDownLatch latch = new CountDownLatch(3);
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);

        pool.submit(() -> { generateReport("Accounting"); latch.countDown(); });
        pool.submit(() -> { generateReport("Loans");      latch.countDown(); });
        pool.submit(() -> { generateReport("Compliance"); latch.countDown(); });

        latch.await(); // blocks until count = 0
        System.out.println("All reports ready. Merging.");
        // latch.await() again → returns immediately (count stays at 0)
        pool.shutdown();
    }

    static void generateReport(String dept) {
        System.out.println(dept + " report done");
    }
}
```

```text
Accounting report done
Loans report done
Compliance report done
All reports ready. Merging.
```

### How does CyclicBarrier work?

CyclicBarrier(N) blocks each of N threads until all N have called `await()`. When the Nth arrives, all are released. The barrier auto-resets for the next trip.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class BarrierDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CyclicBarrier barrier = new CyclicBarrier(3, () ->
            System.out.println("--- All reached checkpoint ---"));
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);

        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            final int id = i;
            pool.submit(() -> {
                try {
                    System.out.println("Auditor-" + id + " phase-1");
                    barrier.await();
                    System.out.println("Auditor-" + id + " phase-2");
                    barrier.await(); // reusable!
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                }
            });
        }
        pool.shutdown();
    }
}
```

```text
Auditor-0 phase-1
Auditor-1 phase-1
Auditor-2 phase-1
--- All reached checkpoint ---
Auditor-0 phase-2
Auditor-1 phase-2
Auditor-2 phase-2
--- All reached checkpoint ---
```

### How do they compare?

| Feature | CountDownLatch | CyclicBarrier |
|---|---|---|
| Reusable | No (one-time) | Yes (auto-resets) |
| Who counts/waits | Different threads OK | Same threads |
| Reset | Cannot reset | Automatic per trip |
| Barrier action | None | Optional Runnable |
| Exception | N/A | BrokenBarrierException |
| Use case | Wait for N events | N threads sync at point |
| Thread count | Waiters != counters | Waiters = counters = N |

### What is BrokenBarrierException?

If a thread waiting at the barrier is interrupted or times out, all other waiting threads receive `BrokenBarrierException`. The barrier is in a broken state and must be reset with `barrier.reset()` before reuse.

### What about Semaphore?

Semaphore limits concurrent access to a resource by issuing a fixed number of permits.

```java
Semaphore dbSemaphore = new Semaphore(5); // max 5 concurrent DB calls
try {
    dbSemaphore.acquire();
    queryDatabase();
} finally {
    dbSemaphore.release();
}
```

### What about Phaser?

Phaser is a flexible synchronizer that supports dynamic registration and multiple phases. It combines features of both CountDownLatch and CyclicBarrier.

### When should you use each?

- **CountDownLatch**: service startup (wait for N components to initialize), test setup (wait for server to be ready), batch completion (wait for N tasks).
- **CyclicBarrier**: multi-phase simulation, parallel batch processing with phase boundaries, synchronized testing.
- **Semaphore**: connection pooling, rate limiting, resource throttling.

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: reusing a triggered CountDownLatch
latch.await(); // count = 0
// ... next day ...
latch.await(); // returns immediately! Need a new latch.

// WRONG: wrong party count in CyclicBarrier
CyclicBarrier barrier = new CyclicBarrier(4); // expects 4 threads
// Only 3 threads call await() → deadlock! 4th never arrives.
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can CountDownLatch count up? No, only down. Use Phaser for dynamic registration.
- What happens if you call countDown() more than N times? Nothing: count stays at zero.
- Can CyclicBarrier be used with fewer threads than the party count? No, it will deadlock waiting for the Nth thread.
